  "work": "To expand $(\\sin x)^7$ in terms of multiple angles, we can use the complex exponential form of sine, $\\sin x = \\frac{e^{ix} - e^{-ix}}{2i}$. Then, $(\\sin x)^7 = \\left(\\frac{e^{ix} - e^{-ix}}{2i}\\right)^7$. Expanding this using the binomial theorem, we get a sum of terms involving $e^{i(7x - 2kx)}$ for $k = 0$ to $7$. We then express each term in terms of sine using $\\sin nx = \\frac{e^{inx} - e^{-inx}}{2i}$, and collect coefficients for each multiple angle $\\sin nx$. Finally, we match the coefficient of $\\sin x$ on both sides of the equation to find $d$.",
  "draft": "To find the value of $d$, we expand $(\\sin x)^7$ using complex exponentials. Recall that $\\sin x = \\frac{e^{ix} - e^{-ix}}{2i}$, so $(\\sin x)^7 = \\left(\\frac{e^{ix} - e^{-ix}}{2i}\\right)^7$. Expanding this using the binomial theorem, we get $\\sum_{k=0}^{7} \\binom{7}{k} (e^{ix})^{7-k} (-e^{-ix})^k (2i)^{-7}$. Simplifying, we get $\\sum_{k=0}^{7} \\binom{7}{k} (-1)^k (2i)^{-7} e^{i(7 - 2k)x}$. Each term can be expressed as a sine function using $\\sin nx = \\frac{e^{inx} - e^{-inx}}{2i}$. After collecting coefficients for $\\sin x$, we find that the coefficient of $\\sin x$ is $\\frac{7}{2^6}$, so $d = \\frac{7}{64}$."
}

